 "drivingQuestion": "Does the mishna's broad use of mazon refute Rav and Shmuel?",
 "outcome": "inconclusive",
 "outcomeNote": "the refutation is raised; discussion continues in the next unit",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "eruvin.30a.8",
   "daf": "30a",
   "sentenceId": 7,
   "he": "הַנּוֹדֵר מִן הַמָּזוֹן, מוּתָּר בַּמַּיִם כּוּ׳. מֶלַח וּמַיִם הוּא דְּלָא אִיקְּרִי מָזוֹן, הָא כׇּל מִילֵּי אִיקְּרִי מָזוֹן. לֵימָא תֶּיהְוֵי תְּיוּבְתָּא דְּרַב וּשְׁמוּאֵל. דְּרַב וּשְׁמוּאֵל דְּאָמְרִי תַּרְוַיְיהוּ: אֵין מְבָרְכִין ״בּוֹרֵא מִינֵי מְזוֹנוֹת״ אֶלָּא עַל חֲמֵשֶׁת הַמִּינִין בִּלְבַד.",
   "en": "We learned in the mishna: «One who vows that nourishment» is prohibited to him «is permitted» to eat «water» and salt. The Gemara infers from this: «It is» only «salt and water» that «are not considered nourishment, but all» other food «items are considered nourishment. Let us say» that «this is a refutation of» the position of «Rav and Shmuel. As it was Rav and Shmuel who both said: One only recites the blessing: Who creates» the various «kinds of nourishment, over the five species» of grain «alone,» but not over other types of food."
  }
 ],
